3. Ground Zero™ — Shared Infrastructure

Ground Zero™ is the proposed foundational virtualization and integration environment.

Its responsibility is to provide common infrastructure without forcing every application into a single execution model.

3.1 Core services

- Service discovery and registration.
- API gateway.
- Authentication and authorization.
- Event distribution.
- Configuration management.
- Version control.
- Observability and monitoring.
- Data validation.
- Cryptographic signing.
- Backup and recovery.
- Development and testing environments.

3.2 Service architecture

Each subsystem should expose documented interfaces.

Recommended service boundaries:

Service| Interface responsibility
Identity API| Accounts, pseudonyms, credentials
Communication API| Messages and channel metadata
Card API| Inventory, rules and game state
Invoice API| Invoice creation and status
Payment API| Payment intents and provider status
Escrow API| Deposits, conditions and release
Auction API| Listings, bids and outcomes
Integrity API| Hashes, signatures and evidence
Analytics API| Aggregated operational measurements

Each service must have an identifiable owner, version, authorization policy, and failure-handling procedure.

3.3 Rational Anti-Concentration Integrity

The architecture should support:

- Replaceable infrastructure providers.
- Multiple authorized administrators where appropriate.
- Independent audit capabilities.
- Documented emergency privileges.
- Portable records and export procedures.
- Open interface specifications.
- Recovery without exclusive dependence on one operator.

Decentralization should be introduced where it provides a demonstrable advantage, not merely as an architectural label.

