10. Tri-Layer Integrity Ledger

Primary role: Documentary integrity and verifiable system history.

The Tri-Layer Integrity Ledger establishes a common evidence architecture.

The following decomposition is the proposed implementation baseline.

10.1 Layer A — Operational Record

Stores authoritative application events and transaction state.

Examples:

- Account creation.
- Message metadata.
- Card transfers.
- Invoice creation.
- Payment status.
- Auction bids.
- Escrow transitions.

10.2 Layer B — Cryptographic Integrity

Provides:

- Cryptographic hashes.
- Digital signatures.
- Event sequence references.
- Merkle-tree commitments where appropriate.
- Timestamp evidence.
- Record versioning.
- Integrity verification.

10.3 Layer C — Independent Evidence

Provides:

- Audit reports.
- External verification.
- Dispute documentation.
- Human review records.
- Reconciliation results.
- Governance decisions.
- Evidence preservation.

10.4 Event record structure

Each material event should include:

Field| Purpose
event_id| Unique event identifier
event_type| Defined event category
entity_id| Associated object
actor_id| Authorized actor reference
timestamp| Recorded event time
sequence| Ordering reference
schema_version| Data contract version
previous_hash| Prior event commitment
payload_hash| Event payload commitment
signature| Authorized cryptographic signature
evidence_ref| Supporting evidence reference

10.5 Cryptographic allocation hashing

Allocation records should identify:

- Source amount or resource.
- Allocation recipient.
- Allocation rule.
- Allocation version.
- Authorization reference.
- Transaction reference.
- Resulting balance.
- Verification hash.

A hash chain provides tamper evidence, not absolute immutability. Protection against alteration also requires access controls, independent checkpoints, backups, and verification.

